<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sewing-machine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sewing machine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sewing machine sews fabric and other materials together with thread. A British patent for a mechanical sewing aid, a double-pointed needle with an eye at one end, was awarded to Charles Wiesenthal in 1755, and Thomas Saint designed a machine in 1790 that was probably never built. In 1804 two Englishmen, Thomas Stone and James Henderson, built a machine. Barthélemy Thimonnier, a French tailor, patented the first practical and widely used one in 1829 and 1830. Walter Hunt made the first American lockstitch machine in 1832, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-howe">Elias Howe</a> and Isaac Merritt Singer built on these designs in 1845 and 1851.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sewing-machine.jpg" alt="Sewing machine" width="330" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BERNINA International AG, Steckborn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:730_sw_ori.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The pharmacy of Emanuel Merck in Darmstadt began industrial production of alkaloids in the 1820s and became a major chemical and pharmaceutical firm, Adam Opel started making <b>sewing machines</b> at Rüsselsheim in 1862 and motor cars in 1899, and Offenbach became known for leather goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1804</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Thomas Stone · James Henderson · Barthélemy Thimonnier · <a href="/encyclopedia/elias-howe">Elias Howe</a> · <a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Austria (1807)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Thomas Saint's design of 1790, generally considered the first sewing machine, was never constructed; Josef Madersperger began work in 1807 and showed a machine in 1814; Thimonnier's of 1829 was the first practical one.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-needle">Sewing needle<span>c. 50,000 years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sewing-machine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
